WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:05.480
Python frameworks provide file-handling patterns, but those patterns still
need deliberate use.

2
00:00:05.480 --> 00:00:10.047
Flask applications may use helpers for sending files or serving uploaded
content.

3
00:00:10.047 --> 00:00:13.832
Django separates static assets, media files, and storage backends.

4
00:00:13.832 --> 00:00:18.790
FastAPI and Starlette offer response classes and integration points for
file responses.

5
00:00:18.790 --> 00:00:26.815
These tools can reduce custom code, but they do not automatically
authorize resources, choose safe storage names, or enforce tenant
boundaries.

6
00:00:26.815 --> 00:00:30.142
Reverse proxies and web servers also influence file safety.

7
00:00:30.142 --> 00:00:35.753
Some deployments serve static assets directly, while application code
controls private downloads.

8
00:00:35.753 --> 00:00:39.081
Some use internal redirects or storage service signed URLs.

9
00:00:39.081 --> 00:00:41.560
Each pattern has a different trust boundary.

10
00:00:41.560 --> 00:00:51.411
The application must ensure that public assets are meant to be public and
that private files require authorization before any web server, proxy, or
storage service can serve them.

11
00:00:51.411 --> 00:00:52.977
Deployment layout matters.

12
00:00:52.977 --> 00:00:59.371
Containers may have read-only filesystems, mounted volumes, writable
temporary directories, or shared storage.

13
00:00:59.371 --> 00:01:03.547
Serverless runtimes may provide temporary storage with lifecycle limits.

14
00:01:03.547 --> 00:01:11.702
Cloud object stores may use keys instead of filesystem paths, but key
construction can still be influenced by unsafe input if the design is
careless.

15
00:01:11.702 --> 00:01:18.292
Environment-specific configuration should be reviewed so development
shortcuts do not become production behavior.

16
00:01:18.292 --> 00:01:26.251
The durable recommendation is to use framework helpers and storage
services as part of a clear storage architecture, not as shortcuts around
it.

17
00:01:26.251 --> 00:01:29.122
Keep storage roots configured, not user-supplied.

18
00:01:29.122 --> 00:01:31.471
Keep public and private content separate.

19
00:01:31.471 --> 00:01:34.015
Keep runtime credentials least-privileged.

20
00:01:34.015 --> 00:01:38.191
Keep direct file reads out of routes unless they are carefully
constrained.

21
00:01:38.191 --> 00:01:42.040
A framework helper is safest when the surrounding design is also safe.
